{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# Time Series Fundamentals Notebook\n\n> Hands-on Build It and Exercises."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Build It"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "The code in `code/time_series.py` implements the core building blocks from scratch."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Lag Feature Creator"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def make_lag_features(series, n_lags):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    n = len(series)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    X = np.full((n, n_lags), np.nan)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    for lag in range(1, n_lags + 1):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        X[lag:, lag - 1] = series[:-lag]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    valid = ~np.isnan(X).any(axis=1)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return X[valid], series[valid]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "This converts a 1D series into a feature matrix where each row has the last `n_lags` values as features, and the current value as the target."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Walk-Forward Cross-Validation"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def walk_forward_split(n_samples, n_splits=5, min_train=50):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    assert min_train < n_samples, \"min_train must be less than n_samples\""
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    step = max(1, (n_samples - min_train) // n_splits)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    for i in range(n_splits):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        train_end = min_train + i * step"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        test_end = min(train_end + step, n_samples)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        if train_end >= n_samples:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "            break"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        yield slice(0, train_end), slice(train_end, test_end)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Each split ensures training data comes strictly before test data. The training window expands with each fold."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Simple Autoregressive Model"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "A pure AR model is just linear regression on lag features:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "class SimpleAR:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    def __init__(self, n_lags=5):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        self.n_lags = n_lags"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        self.weights = None"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        self.bias = None"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    def fit(self, series):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        X, y = make_lag_features(series, self.n_lags)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        # Solve via normal equations"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        X_b = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(X)), X])"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        theta = np.linalg.lstsq(X_b, y, rcond=None)[0]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        self.bias = theta[0]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        self.weights = theta[1:]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        return self"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "This is conceptually identical to linear regression from Lesson 02, but applied to time-lagged versions of the same variable."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Stationarity Check"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "The code computes rolling statistics to visually and numerically assess stationarity:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def check_stationarity(series, window=50):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    rolling_mean = np.array(["
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        series[max(0, i - window):i].mean()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        for i in range(1, len(series) + 1)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    ])"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    rolling_std = np.array(["
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        series[max(0, i - window):i].std()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        for i in range(1, len(series) + 1)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    ])"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return rolling_mean, rolling_std"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "If the rolling mean drifts or the rolling std changes, the series is non-stationary. Apply differencing and check again."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "The code also checks stationarity by comparing the first half and second half of the series. If the means differ by more than half a standard deviation or the variance ratio exceeds 2x, the series is flagged as non-stationary."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Autocorrelation"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def autocorrelation(series, max_lag=20):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    n = len(series)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    mean = series.mean()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    var = series.var()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    acf = np.zeros(max_lag + 1)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    for k in range(max_lag + 1):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        cov = np.mean((series[:n-k] - mean) * (series[k:] - mean))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        acf[k] = cov / var if var > 0 else 0"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return acf"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. **Stationarity experiment.** Generate a series with a linear trend. Check stationarity with rolling statistics. Apply first differencing. Check again. How many rounds of differencing does it take for a quadratic trend?\n\n2. **Lag selection.** Compute ACF on a seasonal series (period=7). Which lags have the highest autocorrelation? Create lag features using only those lags (not consecutive lags). Does accuracy improve compared to using lags 1 through 7?\n\n3. **Walk-forward vs random split.** Train a Ridge regression on lag features. Evaluate with random 80/20 split and with walk-forward validation. How much does the random split overestimate performance?\n\n4. **Feature engineering.** Add rolling mean (window=7), rolling std (window=7), and day-of-week features to the lag features. Compare accuracy with and without these extras using walk-forward validation.\n\n5. **Multi-step forecasting.** Modify the AR model to predict 5 steps ahead instead of 1. Compare two strategies: (a) predict one step, use the prediction as input for the next step (recursive), and (b) train separate models for each horizon (direct). Which is more accurate?"
      ]
    }
